<div class="analysis-bar">
  <p><strong>Menu Psychology:</strong> The G&G (grab-and-go) window captures an uncontested daypart — a distinct meal-time service window — no BBQ competitor in the corridor serves smoked burritos at 7am. <strong>Framing effect (Tversky & Kahneman, 1981):</strong> the first item a guest reads sets the price anchor for the meal. Opening with a $9.49 flat price across all burritos removes decision friction — guests choose the filling, not the price. <strong>Flat pricing psychology (Ariely, 2009):</strong> uniform pricing reduces the cognitive load of comparison and speeds up the transaction, which matters at a 7am grab-and-go window.</p>
