<div class="analysis-grid">

  <div class="analysis-card">
    <div class="ac-title">Menu Engineering Matrix</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Kasavana &amp; Smith (1982)</div>
    <div class="ac-body">Items classified by <strong>popularity</strong> (sales mix) and <strong>contribution margin</strong>. The 2×2 matrix drives every pricing, placement, and promotion decision.</div>
    <table class="matrix-table">
      <tr><th>Category</th><th>Popularity</th><th>Contribution Margin (CM)</th><th>Strategy</th></tr>
      <tr><td>⭐ Star</td><td class="green">High</td><td class="green">High</td><td>Feature · Upsell · Protect</td></tr>
      <tr><td>🐂 Plowhorse</td><td class="green">High</td><td class="amber">Low</td><td>Keep · Cost-manage · Don't feature</td></tr>
      <tr><td>❓ Puzzle</td><td class="red">Low</td><td class="green">High</td><td>Promote · Verbal suggestion · Reposition</td></tr>
      <tr><td>🐕 Dog</td><td class="red">Low</td><td class="red">Low</td><td>Remove at 90-day review</td></tr>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="analysis-card highlight">
    <div class="ac-title">Price Anchoring &amp; the Decoy Effect</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Thaler (1980) · Ariely (2009) · Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</div>
    <div class="ac-body">
      The <strong>Smokehouse Sampler ($46)</strong> is a strategic anchor — its primary purpose is to make the $24 Brisket Plate appear to be excellent value. Guests anchor to the highest price they see first, then compare everything else relative to it.<br><br>
      The <strong>Full Rack ($38) vs. Half Rack ($22)</strong> pairing is a textbook decoy: the Full Rack exists to make the Half Rack feel like a bargain. Most guests land on the Half Rack, believing they've made the smart choice — while the restaurant delivers the same margin profile.<br><br>
      <em>Never list prices with dollar signs in prominent positions</em> — research shows removing the "$" symbol reduces price salience and increases spend (Yang, 2012).
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="analysis-card info">
    <div class="ac-title">Menu Placement &amp; Eye-Tracking</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Yang (2012) · Pavesic (2005) · Gallup &amp; Robinson Research</div>
    <div class="ac-body">
      Restaurant menus follow predictable <strong>eye-tracking patterns</strong>. The top-right corner gets first attention on a two-panel menu; for single-page menus, the first item and the last item in each section receive disproportionate attention.<br><br>
      <strong>Implication:</strong> Place the highest-margin Stars (<em>Pulled Pork Plate, Smoked Chicken, Old Fashioned</em>) first and last within each section. Bury Plowhorses (Ribs) in the middle — guests still find them when they want them, without being nudged toward thin-margin items.<br><br>
      <strong>Section naming matters:</strong> "Delaware Shore Sides" is more memorable — and more justifiable at $4–$5.50 — than "Sides."
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="analysis-card">
    <div class="ac-title">Server Verbal Suggestion Protocol</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Cialdini (2001) · Brown (2019)</div>
    <div class="ac-body">
      Specific verbal suggestions convert at <strong>2.4× the rate</strong> of open-ended questions. Train every server on these exact scripts:<br><br>
      ✦ <em>"The brisket is what we're known for — it smokes overnight."</em><br>
      ✦ <em>"Can I start you with an Old Fashioned? We use Bulleit bourbon."</em><br>
      ✦ <em>"The pulled pork is the pitmaster's recommendation today."</em><br>
      ✦ <em>"We have the only cold-smoked flounder on the Delaware shore."</em><br><br>
      Each of these suggestions targets a <strong>Star or Puzzle item</strong>. Never suggest Plowhorses.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="analysis-card highlight">
    <div class="ac-title">Beverage Attachment Rate</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Laube &amp; Shuster (2012) · NRA (2025)</div>
    <div class="ac-body">
      Beverages are the <strong>single highest-leverage margin category</strong> on the Salt &amp; Smoke menu:<br><br>
      <table class="matrix-table">
        <tr><th>Item</th><th>Food Cost %</th><th>Contribution Margin (CM)</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Sweet Tea</td><td class="green">4%</td><td class="green">$2.88</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Old Fashioned</td><td class="green">18%</td><td class="green">$10.66</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Dogfish IPA</td><td class="amber">21%</td><td class="amber">$6.35</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pulled Pork Plate</td><td class="amber">26%</td><td class="amber">$13.32</td></tr>
      </table><br>
      A table of 4 that orders cocktails instead of soft drinks generates an additional <em>~$30 in contribution margin</em> at essentially zero additional cost. Beverage attachment rate — the percentage of guests who order a drink alongside their food — is the single most trainable revenue lever in the restaurant. The most profitable 30 minutes of staff training possible.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="analysis-card info">
    <div class="ac-title">Regional Identity as Brand Moat</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Kotler, Bowen, Makens &amp; Baloglu (2017) · Emplifi (2025)</div>
    <div class="ac-body">
      Generic BBQ restaurants compete on price. <strong>Salt &amp; Smoke competes on place.</strong> The "Delaware Shore" identity creates a brand moat that a chain cannot replicate and a future competitor cannot easily copy:<br><br>
      ✦ <em>Cold-smoked Atlantic flounder</em> — local sourcing, local story<br>
      ✦ <em>Old Bay seasoning</em> — Chesapeake/Delaware flavor identity<br>
      ✦ <em>Eastern Shore vinegar slaw</em> — Delmarva regional tradition<br>
      ✦ <em>Dogfish Head / Big Oyster / Crooked Hammock</em> — Delaware craft identity<br><br>
      <strong>UGC (user-generated content — guest photos and social posts) multiplier (Emplifi, 2025):</strong> User-generated content converts at 10× the rate of branded content. The flounder po'boy, Millville Mule copper mug, and smoked wing stack are specifically designed to be photographed and shared.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="analysis-card" style="grid-column: 1 / -1;">
    <div class="ac-title">90-Day Menu Review Protocol</div>
    <div class="ac-source">Kasavana &amp; Smith (1982) · Toast POS Analytics</div>
    <div class="ac-body" style="column-count: 2; column-gap: 32px;">
      Toast POS (point-of-sale system) automatically tracks sales mix — how often each item is ordered relative to total covers — and contribution margin by item. At Day 90, pull the Menu Engineering report and apply the matrix:<br><br>
      <strong>Actions by category:</strong><br>
      ⭐ <em>Stars</em> — Protect. Increase price test by $1–2 if demand is inelastic. Never remove.<br>
      🐂 <em>Plowhorses</em> — Reduce portion slightly (test first), negotiate protein cost, or raise price $1–2. If still underperforming after 2 quarters, reposition or replace.<br>
      ❓ <em>Puzzles</em> — Double down on verbal server suggestions. Add a table tent. If still low popularity at Day 180, consider bundling with a Star (e.g., "Brisket &amp; Flounder Flight").<br>
      🐕 <em>Dogs</em> — Remove from menu at Day 90 review. Exception: if a Dog serves a specific segment (vegetarian option) that drives a companion's Star order, keep it for table-level logic.<br><br>
      <strong>Watch list items:</strong> Delaware Shore Flounder (plate and po'boy formats) are high-margin Puzzles that require active verbal promotion. Pecan Pie is the only current Dog candidate. Garden Smoke Veggie Burrito (G&G — grab-and-go) — reassess at Day 90; remove if &lt;2 units/day.
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
